{
  "courseSlug": "understanding-sector-rotation-and-business-cycles",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Chapter 4: Does Sector Rotation Actually Work in India?",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "Relative Strength: Measuring Sector Leadership With Ratio Charts",
          "slug": "relative-strength-measuring-sector-leadership-with-ratio-charts",
          "body": [
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k001",
              "_type": "bigIdea",
              "text": "A price chart tells you whether a sector went up. A relative strength chart tells you whether owning it beat simply owning the market, which is the question sector rotation is actually trying to answer."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k002",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k002s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Throughout Chapter 4 you have seen relative strength lines and relative returns. This lesson teaches you to build and read them yourself, add a simple trend and momentum filter, and avoid the most common trap: reading one timeframe and ignoring the other. Every number comes from NSE month-end closing values.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k003",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k003s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "What a Ratio Chart Measures",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k004",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{RS}_t = \\frac{\\text{Sector index}_t}{\\text{Nifty 50}_t} \\qquad \\text{Rebased RS}_t = \\frac{\\text{RS}_t}{\\text{RS}_{\\text{start}}} \\times 100",
              "caption": "Rebasing to 100 at a start date makes the line easy to read: 120 means the sector has beaten the Nifty 50 by 20% since the start"
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k005",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k005s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Relative strength strips out the market. That is why it can rise while prices fall. In 2026 up to 15 September, Nifty PSU Bank fell 4.4%, but the Nifty 50 fell 11.5%, so PSU bank relative strength rose about 8.1%. An investor who owned PSU banks instead of the index lost money, but lost far less.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k006",
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "Reading price and relative strength together",
              "headers": [
                "Sector price",
                "Relative strength",
                "What it means"
              ],
              "rows": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k006r1",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Rising",
                    "Rising",
                    "Genuine leadership: the sector is winning in absolute and relative terms"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k006r2",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Falling",
                    "Rising",
                    "Defensive leadership: falling less than the market, often seen in sell-offs"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k006r3",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Rising",
                    "Falling",
                    "Riding the market: gains, but owning the index would have done better"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k006r4",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Falling",
                    "Falling",
                    "Genuine laggard: losing in both absolute and relative terms"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k007",
              "_type": "exercise",
              "variant": "numericInput",
              "title": "Build a Relative Strength Reading",
              "prompt": "Nifty Metal closed at 8,649.9 on 31 December 2024 and 13,193.9 at the end of August 2026. The Nifty 50 closed at 23,644.8 and 24,080.4 on the same dates. By what percentage did metal relative strength rise over the period? Round to one decimal place.",
              "correctValue": 49.8,
              "tolerance": 0.2,
              "unit": "%",
              "explanation": "Metal returned +52.5% and the Nifty 50 +1.8%. Relative strength is (13193.9 / 8649.9) divided by (24080.4 / 23644.8), which is about 1.498, a rise of 49.8%. Notice this is not the same as simply subtracting the two returns (50.7 pp); the ratio compounds correctly."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k008",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k008s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Trend: Is Leadership Building or Fading?",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k009",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k009s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "A raw ratio line wiggles every month. The simplest way to see the trend is to compare it with its own moving average. When relative strength is above its average and the average is rising, leadership is established. When it drops below a falling average, leadership is fading.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
