            {
              "_key": "c4l4k014",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k014s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Nifty IT is the perfect warning. Its relative strength fell from 100 at the end of 2024 to a low of 60.1 in Jun 2026. Then it bounced, and by the end of August 2026 it stood at 70.7, above its 6-month average. On a short-term view, IT looks like it is turning into a leader. On a 12-month view, it still lagged the Nifty 50 by 10.1%. A bounce inside a long downtrend is not the same as a change in leadership.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k015",
              "_type": "callout",
              "type": "warning",
              "text": "Always read relative strength on at least two timeframes, for example a 6-month trend and a 12-month trend. A short-term signal that disagrees with the longer trend is a reason to watch, not to act. Many false rotation calls come from treating a relief rally in a long-lagging sector as a new leadership cycle."
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k016",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "h2",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k016s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Adding Momentum: The Rotation Quadrants",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k017",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k017md2",
                  "_type": "glossaryTerm",
                  "definition": "A chart created by Julius de Kempenaer that plots many securities by the trend of their relative strength (JdK RS-Ratio, horizontal axis) and the momentum of that trend (JdK RS-Momentum, vertical axis), both centred on 100, dividing them into four quadrants."
                }
              ],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k017s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Trend tells you where relative strength is; momentum tells you where it is heading. Plotting both creates a ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k017s2",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Relative Rotation Graph",
                  "marks": [
                    "c4l4k017md2"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k017s3",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": ", developed by Julius de Kempenaer. Sectors tend to move clockwise through four quadrants: leading, weakening, lagging, and improving.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k018",
              "_type": "table",
              "caption": "The four rotation quadrants",
              "headers": [
                "Quadrant",
                "Relative strength trend",
                "Momentum of that trend",
                "Typical reading"
              ],
              "rows": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k018r1",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Leading",
                    "Above 100",
                    "Above 100",
                    "Strong and still strengthening"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k018r2",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Weakening",
                    "Above 100",
                    "Below 100",
                    "Still strong, but losing momentum; often the next step towards lagging"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k018r3",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Lagging",
                    "Below 100",
                    "Below 100",
                    "Weak and still weakening"
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k018r4",
                  "_type": "tableRow",
                  "cells": [
                    "Improving",
                    "Below 100",
                    "Above 100",
                    "Still weak, but momentum turning up; where new leaders often first appear"
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k019",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k019s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "The official JdK RS-Ratio and RS-Momentum calculations are proprietary. You can approximate the idea with two simple steps on monthly data.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k020",
              "_type": "mathBlock",
              "latex": "\\text{RS Trend}_t = \\frac{\\text{RS}_t}{\\text{6-month average of RS}} \\times 100 \\qquad \\text{RS Momentum}_t = \\frac{\\text{RS Trend}_t}{\\text{RS Trend}_{t-1}} \\times 100",
              "caption": "A simplified teaching version, not the official Relative Rotation Graph formula"
            },
