            {
              "_key": "c4l4k022",
              "_type": "block",
              "style": "normal",
              "markDefs": [],
              "children": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k022s1",
                  "_type": "span",
                  "text": "Read the table with the timeframe trap in mind. Defence, pharma, PSU banks, and metals sit in the leading quadrant with strongly positive 12-month relative returns, consistent leadership on both timeframes. FMCG, energy, and infrastructure are lagging. IT and realty are the interesting cases: both show short-term strength after sharp falls, yet IT is still negative over 12 months, and realty has already slipped into the weakening quadrant. This is a snapshot at one month end, and quadrant positions can change quickly.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "_key": "c4l4k026",
              "_type": "collapsible",
              "title": "Go deeper: the Mansfield relative strength indicator",
              "content": [
                {
                  "_key": "c4l4k023",
                  "_type": "block",
                  "style": "normal",
                  "markDefs": [],
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "_key": "c4l4k023s1",
                      "_type": "span",
                      "text": "Stan Weinstein popularised a variant known as Mansfield relative strength, used on weekly charts. It turns the ratio into an oscillator around zero by comparing relative strength with its own 52-week moving average.",
